SURAH = "al-Anbiyāʾ"
NUM = 21

BLOCKS = {

18: ("Falsehood's Lack of Substance", [
 "The verse's image implies something about falsehood's nature, not only about its fate. What collapses on contact has no substance of its own, and the collapse is therefore immediate rather than gradual.",
 "This is why the sequence casting, crushing, vanishing occupies a single clause. There is no prolonged struggle to narrate, because falsehood offers no sustained resistance.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī's note that vanishes is understood by some to mean is destroyed reinforces the point: the outcome is annihilation, and the particle behold marks how quickly it arrives.",
 "The third sense he records, of an object stamping a form into softer material, adds the converse claim. What yields to truth is not merely erased but takes truth's shape, which is why the softer material must be the falsehood.",
 "Read against 34:49, where falsehood originates naught nor restores, the verse's claim becomes precise: falsehood cannot create and cannot repair, so it has nothing with which to resist.",
 "At 42:24 the instrument is specified as God's Words, which places the casting in Divine speech rather than in human argument, and answers the sūrah's opponents on their own ground.",
 "The opponents claimed the Prophet ﷺ was a poet or a sorcerer, and the verse responds that what he brings is truth cast by God, which no human craft can match or withstand.",
 "The closing woe then applies the claim. Those who described God falsely are warned not because they lost an argument but because what they set against truth had no substance to begin with.",
]),

19: ("The Order the Verse Describes", [
 "The verse sets out a hierarchy rather than issuing a command, and the hierarchy runs from universal ownership through angelic nearness to unceasing worship.",
 "The ownership is asserted without exception: whosoever is in the heavens and on the earth belongs to God. Nothing is excluded, and the exclusion of nothing is what the following clauses build on.",
 "Al-Rāzī and al-Ṭabarī identify those who are with Him as the angels, and note that the phrase admits the rendering those who are near Him, which avoids implying spatial accompaniment.",
 "The parallel at 41:38, cited by the commentators, treats angelic worship in similar terms and supports reading the two passages together rather than in isolation.",
 "The angels' defining feature is negative: they are not too arrogant to worship. The sūrah has just attributed arrogance to those who describe God falsely, so the contrast is exact.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī glosses nor do they weary as neither tiring nor becoming bored, which covers exhaustion and ennui together, and the pairing is what makes the constancy remarkable.",
 "Al-Rāzī's qualification that such worship is possible only for angels and not for human beings keeps the verse descriptive rather than prescriptive, and the keeping matters.",
 "The comparison recorded by al-Ṭabarī, that glorification is for the angels what breathing is for human beings, explains the constancy: what is constitutive does not exhaust the one who does it.",
 "The verse therefore describes creatures for whom worship is nature, and the description sets a standard the sūrah elsewhere applies to human beings under very different conditions.",
]),

110: ("What the Verse Removes", [
 "The verse's argument works by elimination. It removes the possibility that concealment offers cover, and the removal is what the parallel structure achieves.",
 "The first clause establishes that open speech is known. Left alone it would permit the inference that hidden speech is not, and the second clause exists to block that inference.",
 "The shift to the second person in the second clause is what makes the block effective. The addressees are told that what they conceal is known, not that concealment is generally visible.",
 "The commentators cite 2:284, 4:149, 27:74, 33:54, and 87:7 to establish that this equality is settled Qur'anic teaching, and the fivefold recurrence indicates a sustained concern.",
 "The consequence is practical rather than speculative. If private deliberation carries no protection, the addressees' plotting gains nothing, and the verse leaves them with that.",
 "The placement after verse 109 is what gives the verse its edge. The Prophet ﷺ has just confessed that he does not know whether the promise is near or far.",
 "His knowledge is limited and expressly so; God's is comprehensive and expressly so, and the two statements together mark the boundary between the messenger's role and the Divine one.",
 "The eschatological sequence at verses 96 to 104 already described the reckoning, so the connection between what is known and what will be accounted for is assumed rather than argued.",
 "The verse therefore gathers the sūrah's eschatological material into one assertion, and the assertion is what the final prayer then rests upon.",
]),

111: ("Respite That Cannot Be Read", [
 "The verse's warning is that the delay cannot be interpreted as evidence, and the warning is what follows from the disclaimer that precedes it.",
 "The Prophet ﷺ does not know whether the promise is near or far, and the addressees cannot know whether the respite is favour or trial, so neither side holds the certainty the situation seems to offer.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī's gloss is the hinge: the delaying of the punishment is a trial and not a blessing, which reverses the natural reading of a reprieve.",
 "He supports the gloss with 26:205 to 207, where the Qur'an says that additional time only gives disbelievers further opportunity to commit evil and to entrench themselves in denial.",
 "The support shows the reading is not a local invention but part of a wider treatment of delayed punishment, and the commentators cite the parallel to establish exactly that.",
 "Al-Rāzī preserves the alternative tied to the fair-warning reading of ʿalā sawāʾ at verse 109, on which the verse refers to a coming fight announced in advance rather than to a moral test.",
 "The two readings differ in kind, one concerning the addressees' spiritual condition and the other their political situation, and neither is subordinated to the other.",
 "The closing phrase an enjoyment for a while is common to both, and its insistence on temporariness is what the verse leaves standing whichever reading is adopted.",
 "The temporariness is the verse's residue: whatever the delay means, it is bounded, and the bounding is what the addressees are left to reckon with.",
]),

112: ("The Prayer as the Sūrah's Ending", [
 "The sūrah closes with a supplication rather than a conclusion, and the choice is what the verse's structure makes possible.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī notes the verb admits an imperative reading, Say, my Lord, judge with truth, and a declarative one, my Lord has judged or judges with truth, and the two leave the final note poised between petition and confidence.",
 "He supports the declarative reading with 7:89, where the prayer is our Lord, decide between us and our people in truth, and Thou art the best of deciders, which establishes the idiom.",
 "The parallel also shows that asking God to decide in truth is a recognized prophetic prayer, so the verse ends the sūrah within a tradition of supplication rather than outside it.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī records two objects for that which you ascribe: accusations that the Prophet ﷺ is a sorcerer or a poet, with the cross-reference to verses 3 to 5, and blasphemous claims about God such as His having a child, citing 19:35.",
 "The two differ in whether the slander is aimed at the messenger or at God, and the commentators keep both without preferring either.",
 "The assertion our Lord is the Compassionate is what the request for judgment rests upon, and it gathers the mercy that runs through the sūrah from the prophets' deliverances to verse 107.",
 "The description of God as the one whose help is sought completes the movement, and the seeking is directed at the very ascriptions the sūrah has contested since its opening verses.",
 "The sūrah thus ends where it began, with the dispute over what is said about God and His messenger, but ends it by appeal rather than by argument.",
]),

}
